From: Steam-Link-PS5
Subject: control: size the frame window to hold the Big Picture startup burst

The second intermittent-startup failure: the session authenticated, started the
audio channel, then logged dozens of "Frames window overflow" and disconnected
("THE STREAM ENDED") before the video channel (channel 4) was ever added - a
black screen where Big Picture never appeared, exactly the user's report.

The control channel's frame window was 128 slots. Control frames are reliable
and delivered strictly in order, so a single lost reliable packet - or one lost
fragment of a large config message, of which Big Picture's initial state sync
sends many - stalls the window head (IHS_SessionPacketsWindowPoll refuses to
advance past a gap). While the head waits for the host to resend the missing
packet, the rest of the burst keeps arriving and piles up behind it. At 128
slots the window filled in under a second; the next packet tripped the
"too far ahead" branch of IHS_SessionPacketsWindowAdd, which returns false, and
OnControlReceived treats that as fatal (IHS_SessionDisconnect). Captured in a
relaunch loop: 1 in ~12 launches overflowed this way.

The gap does heal on its own - the host retransmits the un-ACKed packet on its
sender-driven reliable-delivery timer, and our ACKs already go out redundantly
on a lossy uplink (patch 0013) - after which the entire backlog drains in order.
The window just needs room to wait. Raise it to 2048 slots (matching the video
window); the startup burst fits with margin. Overflow now fires only if 2048
packets accumulate without the gap ever healing - a genuinely dead link - where
disconnecting so the launcher can retry is the correct backstop.

--- a/src/session/channels/ch_control.c
+++ b/src/session/channels/ch_control.c
@@ -138,7 +138,19 @@
 static void OnControlInit(IHS_SessionChannel *channel, const void *data) {
     IHS_UNUSED(data);
     IHS_SessionChannelControl *control = (IHS_SessionChannelControl *) channel;
-    control->framePacketWindow = IHS_SessionPacketsWindowCreate(128);
+    // 128 slots could not hold the Big Picture startup burst while a single lost
+    // reliable packet healed. Control frames are delivered strictly in order, so
+    // one missing packet (or one missing fragment of a large config message)
+    // stalls the window head until the host resends it; meanwhile the rest of the
+    // burst piles up. At 128 the window filled in under a second and overflowed,
+    // tripping IHS_SessionDisconnect before the video channel was ever added - the
+    // intermittent black screen where Big Picture never launched. The host resends
+    // the un-ACKed packet on its own timer (sender-driven reliable delivery) and
+    // the whole backlog then drains in order, so the fix is simply room to wait:
+    // 2048 slots (matching the video window) buffers the entire burst. Overflow
+    // now only fires if 2048 packets pile up without the gap healing - a genuinely
+    // dead link - where ending the stream cleanly is the right backstop.
+    control->framePacketWindow = IHS_SessionPacketsWindowCreate(2048);
 }

 static void OnControlDeinit(IHS_SessionChannel *channel) {
